Combining the MA method with SPS would be very attractive. MA enables the alloying of different elemental powders at room temperature, producing a homogeneous solid solution with a refined grain structure and enhanced solid solubility, which is challenging to achieve through LSP. SPS with rapid consolidation of powders using pulsed electrical current and uniaxial pressure, reaching nearly the theoretical density at lower temperatures within minutes while preventing grain growth and maintaining microstructure. The combination of MA and SPS offers several advantages, including cost savings, high performance, fast processing, ultra-fine grain refinement (nanoscale), uniform microstructure, reduced porosity, and improved mechanical properties. The high heating and cooling rate reduce unwanted phase formation (Because it does not provide enough time for diffusion, unwanted reactions and precipitation) and segregation, making it effective, especially for achieving designed single-phase or multiphase microstructures [ 175 ,  176 ,  179 ,  180 ]. The flexibility and superior control over microstructure and phases offered by MA + SPS explain why combining MA and SPS remains the favored method for fabricating high-performance HEAs. Table  6  presents alloying and sintering conditions of several alloys fabricated by the MA + SPS method, along with key findings.
