Gibbs Free Energy is the most basic thermodynamic criterion for predicting the stability of different phases in HEAs. This parameter carries energy-based information that is crucial for phase formation and analysis of thermodynamic stability. Not every combination of elements will form a solid solution. To evaluate the stability of competing compounds, they should ideally be compared by their Gibbs free energy [ 289 ,  290 – 291 ]. Negative values of this parameter indicate the stable phase, while positive values indicate unstable phases. Numerous studies have shown that minimizing the Gibbs Free Energy when designing HEAs influences the microstructure and phase transformations, properties, and serves as a criterion for predicting the most stable phases [ 9 ,  292 ,  293 – 294 ]. The CALPHAD method also relies on this fact [ 295 ]. In classical thermodynamics, Gibbs free energy (G) is defined as a function of enthalpy (H), absolute temperature (T), and entropy (S), and is mathematically represented by Eq. ( 1 ) [ 244 ]: 1 G = H - T · S \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
