The possibility of stable phase formation and solid solution stabilization concept is the answer to the question of whether the entropic term can overcome the enthalpy term or not. Since ΔG (Eq.  2 ) itself depends on entropy, enthalpy, and temperature, to determine the competition of the entropic term (T m ·ΔS mix ) to mixing enthalpy (ΔH mix ), Yang and Zhang introduced an empirical parameter (Ω) which estimates the possibility of forming a solid solution in HEAs systems. While ΔH mix  typically possesses a greater absolute value in order of kJ/mol, ΔS mix  has a value in order of J/mol; therefore, the entropic term (T m ·ΔS mix ) becomes increasingly significant at higher temperatures. In these situations, the entropic contribution can counterbalance the energetic barrier posed by ΔH mix , suggesting that the driving force for SS formation equals the thermodynamic resistance (i.e., ΔH mix ). This thermodynamic equilibrium facilitates the emergence of SS before the formation of IM or complex phases. To predict the probability of SS formation, the Ω parameter, which is affected by both entropic and enthalpy terms, has been introduced according to [ 244 ] Eq. ( 7 ): 7 Ω = T m Δ S mix Δ H mix \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
