Another factor affecting the stability of SS is the difference in atomic electronegativity of elements, commonly quantified using the Pauling scale. The concept of electronegativity, introduced by Linus Pauling in 1932, describes an atom’s capacity to attract and hold electrons [ 309 ]. According to Hume–Rothery rules for SS formation, the constituent elements should possess similar electronegativity values to minimize the possibility of intermetallic compound formation [ 310 ]. Extending this to HEAs, a minor electronegativity difference among the constituent elements generally enhances SS stability by promoting more uniform bonding, which reduces the tendency for phase ordering or IM formation. Conversely, a larger electronegativity difference destabilizes the SS and can encourage electron transfer between atoms, leading to phase segregation. HEAs with slight electronegativity differences deliver greater thermal stability and reduced exposure to phase transitions at high temperatures, and facilitate the formation of SS phases [ 297 ,  311 ]. In multicomponent systems, the electronegativity deviation is expressed by Eq. ( 10 ), which incorporates the atomic fraction and Pauling electronegativity of each element [ 312 ]. 10 Δ X = ∑ i = 1 n x i ( e i - ∑ / P h a n t o m 0 e i . x i ) 2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
