Developing materials with lower weight while maintaining mechanical properties and performance is a vital and exciting area of advanced materials engineering, particularly in aerospace, automotive, and energy sectors, where improved fuel efficiency, payload capacity, and mechanical performance are essential [ 314 ,  315 ]. The conventional Al, Ti, and Mg-based alloys possess good strength-to-weight ratios but tend to have weaknesses in thermal stability, corrosion protection, or plasticity. HEAs provide a new prospect of lightweight structural materials. By incorporating lightweight elements such as Al, Ti, Mg, Li, and Be, along with structure-strengthening elements like V, Cr, or Mn, into HEA systems, it is possible to achieve a balance between lightweight and improved mechanical performance [ 316 ,  317 ,  318 – 319 ]. Recent research has revealed that lightweight HEAs can exhibit high specific strength, thermal stability, and even functional properties such as shape memory or magnetic behavior, making them attractive for next-generation lightweight engineering materials [ 274 ]. Most reported lightweight HEAs fall within the 2–7 g/cm 3  density range, with compositions tailored to balance density, phase stability, and mechanical properties. In designing lightweight HEAs, theoretical density is calculated using the rule of mixtures theory, Eq. ( 11 ) [ 316 ]. The densities of common elements are listed in Table  12 . 11 ρ th = ∑ i = 1 n x i × A i ∑ i = 1 n x i × A i ρ i \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
