Designing based on phase formation rules and single-phase solid solution stabilizing HEA’s design is more than just the simple mixing of elements. It requires knowledge of atomic interactions, phase formation rules, and the use of predictive tools that incorporate thermodynamic parameters (ΔG mix , ΔS mix , ΔH mix , Ω, and T m ), as well as the Hume-Rothery rules (δ, VEC, Δχ). They enable the selective development of alloys, minimize trial-and-error experimental work, facilitate efficient compositional screening, and allow for tailoring the microstructures and optimal levels of desirable properties in designed alloys. Minimizing Gibbs free energy stabilizes a single-phase solid solution (SS) and suppresses the formation of undesirable phases such as intermetallic compounds. In fact, ΔG mix  is the connecting link between the selection of elements, the microstructure formation, and the control of mechanical properties. The competition between the entropic term and the enthalpy of mixing has a significant influence on the stability of solid solutions in HEAs. The Ω indicator is used to identify these competitors. ΔH mix  ≈ 0 is suitable for the formation of a single-phase solid solution. Very positive values lead to phase separation, and negative values lead to the formation of intermetallic phases. Significant differences in the atomic radii of elements cause instability in the solid solution and may lead to phase separation or the formation of intermetallic compounds. The value of VEC affects the formation of the crystal structure. Only specific compositions can form FCC and BCC solid solutions. The phase formation rules and critical ranges for the solid solutions are shown in Table  11 , and the associated design parameters are detailed in Table  12 .
